WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.370
It's Stephanie Wong, back again.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.200
I'm a cloud developer
advocate, and I

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.640
am thrilled because we have Eric
Brewer, our Google fellow, here

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.080
with me again today.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.820
We are doing a series of videos.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.610
And we are talking
about Google Kubernetes

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.500
Engine for next-gen developers.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.540
Eric, welcome back.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:18.790
ERIC BREWER: Great to be here.

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:19.470
Thank you.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.678
STEPHANIE WONG: I want to
talk about some of the most

00:00:21.678 --> 00:00:25.380
common questions that both you
and I, and I think a lot of us

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.010
here at Google Cloud, have
gotten, the first being, what

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.800
is the difference
between Kubernetes-- that

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.898
is, Google Kubernetes Engine
in particular-- and serverless?

00:00:35.898 --> 00:00:37.690
ERIC BREWER: Well,
they're both the future.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.210
So in that sense, they're
easy to get confused.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:41.910
But I would say
the main difference

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.640
is Kubernetes, and
GKE in particular,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.650
are a really great
platform for building

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.297
a wide range of services.

00:00:48.297 --> 00:00:49.380
And that's really the key.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:50.838
You can build all
kinds of services

00:00:50.838 --> 00:00:53.160
on top of Kubernetes and GKE.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.070
Serverless, especially
classically defined,

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.570
obviously, means no servers.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.030
But it usually means
functions as a service

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:04.650
or request-reply patterns
in particular websites.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.350
And so that is an
example, one of many,

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.630
of a domain-specific solution.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.300
And in general, when you have
a domain-specific solution

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.540
like Cloud Run, you
could do a fantastic job

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:20.460
for that domain that is much
easier to use than Kubernetes.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.340
So if you know exactly what you
want to do and it's opinionated

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.950
and it's narrow, you should
build that as a Platform

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:26.955
as a Service.

00:01:26.955 --> 00:01:28.830
If you're going to build
all kinds of things,

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.930
like your enterprise, Kubernetes
is the great base for that.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.110
And in fact, I see lots
of enterprises building

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.260
internal Platforms as a
Service of various kinds on top

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:38.160
of Kubernetes.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.130
And that combination makes
a huge amount of sense.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.950
STEPHANIE WONG: So it sounds
like if you have a specific use

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.130
case that serverless products
are well-equipped for, then,

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.690
absolutely, go with that.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.540
But what about in
the case where you

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.300
might start with a serverless
product, like Cloud Run, Cloud

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:59.400
Functions, et cetera, and then
you decide later on that we are

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.800
fully bought into
Kubernetes and we would

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.570
like to broaden our use cases?

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.730
We want to build
platforms on top.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.933
Is it possible to have
both in your environment?

00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:10.350
ERIC BREWER: Lots
of customers use

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:14.153
both today in very
interesting combinations.

00:02:14.153 --> 00:02:15.570
There are certainly
customers that

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.582
started out on App
Engine or Cloud Run

00:02:17.582 --> 00:02:19.290
and have moved to
Kubernetes because they

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.210
want finer grained control
over all the details.

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:23.110
That's one reason.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.680
And in fact, they're rebuilding
a customized platform

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.002
internally for themselves.

00:02:27.002 --> 00:02:28.710
There are other groups
that use something

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.780
like Cloud Run for
the front-end services

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.760
and use Kubernetes
for back-end services.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.740
That also makes a lot
of sense because you

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.740
can do a lot of data management
control in Kubernetes.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.470
There are groups-- I
know of at least one

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.277
that uses both for
different reasons, which

00:02:42.277 --> 00:02:43.860
is they have a
Kubernetes cluster that

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.930
does their main load.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.930
But when they have a hot
spot, like a Black Friday,

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.870
they actually use Cloud
Run to absorb the spikes.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.770
And so there's lots of ways
to make this work, really,

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.560
depending on what
cleverness you want

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.118
to play to mix these together.

00:03:00.118 --> 00:03:02.160
But they're definitely--
they work well together.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.620
And you can start on Cloud
Run and move to Kubernetes

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.690
later-- is another
really common pattern.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.410
STEPHANIE WONG: Well,
here's a thought.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.320
So we've talked about
this a little bit

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.740
in our previous videos--
about how platform teams want

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.410
to use Kubernetes,
and yet they also

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.960
want to customize their
platforms for their needs.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.900
We talked about policy
enforcement as an example.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.150
So how should an enterprise
use Kubernetes Engine?

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.960
ERIC BREWER: Well, in
general, a cloud provider

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.420
offers more things
than you, really, would

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.495
like your developers to use.

00:03:35.495 --> 00:03:37.620
And what I mean by that is
if you're an enterprise,

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.400
you have a platform
team, and you say, oh,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.190
we're building this kind
of financial service,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.550
we really don't want to
give our internal developers

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:48.968
all possibilities.

00:03:48.968 --> 00:03:51.510
We don't really want them to be
in charge of their own credit

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.140
card transactions, for example.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:54.492
That's a highly regulated thing.

00:03:54.492 --> 00:03:56.700
It needs to be done very
carefully with careful audit

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:57.540
logs.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.190
And so really,
what I'd like to do

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.590
is give my internal
developers a platform

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.110
where they can do credit
card transactions that

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.170
are guaranteed by the
structure of the services

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.020
to be compliant with all
regulations-- and by the way,

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.310
in all different countries
in which we operate.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.070
And so I see
Kubernetes as the way

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:17.130
to build these customized
platforms, where they enforce

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.220
whatever rules the enterprise
cares about through things

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.170
like control over
project creation,

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.080
control over the nodes you use,
control over the OS you use,

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.860
control over the libraries
and repos you pull from.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.140
Those kinds of
background controls

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.700
are things that
are not typically

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:37.440
up to the internal developers to
decide, but, at the same time,

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.100
provides services framework,
possibly something like Anthos

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.920
Service Mesh to manage
services across these things

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.300
and give their
developers, again,

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:49.630
the productivity that you want
and the velocity of features,

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:51.390
but, again, in a
way that's decoupled

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.060
from risk around certain
features that are highly

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.902
regulated, especially.

00:04:55.902 --> 00:04:57.360
STEPHANIE WONG:
Yeah, and it sounds

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.580
like there is very
much a culture

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.310
and organizational structure
in place in which you

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:04.980
have your operators
and administrators

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.800
and your developers.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.420
Now, we do have a
lot of curiosity

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.900
out there in the
community about advice

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.630
for these next generation
of Kubernetes developers.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:18.060
And we've actually
collected some of them.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.310
I know that you threw
out a prompt for AMA

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.640
questions on your Twitter,
and so did I. So get excited,

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:24.220
everyone.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:27.540
We are going to come back in
the next video with a handful

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:28.660
of those questions.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:29.640
So stay tuned.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:30.822
And thank you so much, Eric.

00:05:30.822 --> 00:05:32.280
ERIC BREWER: Looking
forward to it.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.100
Thank you.

